      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "To evaluate therapeutic results till 5 years after therapy of obstructive pyelonephritis (OPN) emphasizing regular follow-up."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
            "content": "During 5 years, 57 patients with OPN were treated. The patients' charts were reviewed retrospectively for clinical data. These were completed by a questionnaire."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In the group of 57 patients (average age 56 years), about two third were women. Urolithiasis (65%) and tumors (21%) were the main causes of obstruction; fever (91%) and loin pain (86%) the main symptoms. Three fourth of the patients showed renal insufficiency and nearly 50% anemia. E. coli and Proteus spp. were the dominating organisms. Sonography detected obstruction in 93% cases. In one third of cases, CT scan was added; 81% percutaneous nephrostomy and 19% ureteral stenting were the initial methods of urinary drainage. During therapy, 23% nephrectomies (19% complete, 4% partial) were performed. Long-term follow-up showed 11% recurrent OPN and 33% recurrent UTI."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "After diagnosis of OPN, primary nephrostomy or ureteral stenting and antibiotic therapy are the first measures. If recurrent urinary tract infections or OPN occur, long-term follow-up and low-dose antibiotic prophylaxis may be discussed."
          }
        ]
      },
